  Deriving means there's no notification state to get out of sync with tasks — delete a task and its alert vanishes for
  free, and the whole system becomes one pure function you can test by passing in a fake clock. Storing records would
  only earn its cost if you needed notification history, which you don't. Cheap to reverse if that changes.

  Rules: a task notifies only if [38;5;153m!done[39m and [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is set. [38;5;153moverdue[39m when [38;5;153mnow > dueAt[39m; [38;5;153mdue-soon[39m when [38;5;153mnow >= dueAt - 60min[39m.
  Overdue supersedes due-soon, so you never see both for one task.

  [1m4. Scheduling and delivery[0m

  Tick every 30s, plus on page load, plus on [38;5;153mvisibilitychange[39m (so a tab left open overnight is correct the moment you
  look at it).

  - [1mPanel[0m shows all active, undismissed notifications. This is the source of truth and always accurate.
  - [1mToast[0m fires only for notifications that are [3mnewly[0m active since the last tick. On first load, nothing toasts —
    otherwise opening the app after a week would throw a wall of banners at you. The catch-up batch appears in the panel
    with a count badge instead.
  - Dismiss writes a dismissal and removes it from the panel.

  [1m5. Testing[0m

  Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m — zero dependencies, consistent with no build step. Unit tests cover [38;5;153mrules.evaluate[39m against
  an injected clock (due-soon boundary, overdue boundary, done tasks, null due dates, overdue-supersedes-due-soon,
  dismissal matching and the due-date-changed resurface case) and a [38;5;153mstorage[39m round-trip.

  [1m6. Explicitly out of scope[0m

  OS/browser notifications, push, email, multi-user, snooze, stale and explicit-reminder triggers, task editing beyond
  create/complete. All noted in the spec as deferred.

  ---

  Does this look right? If yes, I'll write it to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-15-task-notifications-design.md[39m for your
  review before any code gets written.

